# KSI-CNA-RNT: Azure NSG - Network Security Group Exists and Active
# CIS Azure 6.1-6.4: Network security group restrictions

META
    esp_id `ksi-cna-rnt-az-nsg-baseline-001`
    version `1.0.0`
    dsl_schema_version `1.0.0`
    platform `azure`
    criticality `high`

    title `KSI-CNA-RNT: Azure NSG - network security group provisioned`
    description `Validates that the network security group is provisioned and has security rules configured to restrict network traffic, ensuring machine-based resources are not exposed without access controls`
    author `security-team`
    tags `azure,nsg,network,firewall,access-control,CIS-6.1,CIS-6.2`

    control_mapping `KSI:KSI-CNA-RNT,NIST:SC-7,NIST:AC-4`
    control_objective `KSI-CNA-RNT`

    assessment_method `TEST`
    implementation_status `implemented`
    responsible_role `cloud-admin`

    control_origination `sp-system`
    customer_responsibility `configuration`

    remediation_days `3`
    recommendation `Ensure NSGs are attached to all subnets and NICs, with explicit deny rules for unnecessary inbound traffic`

    impl_KSI_CNA_RNT `Network traffic restriction is validated by confirming the network security group exists (found=true) and is successfully provisioned (provisioningState=Succeeded). The NSG must have at least one security rule defined (security_rule_count>=1) to enforce traffic filtering. Network security groups are the primary mechanism for controlling inbound and outbound traffic to Azure virtual network resources, implementing microsegmentation and defense-in-depth at the network layer.`

META_END

DEF
    OBJECT nsg_demo
        name `nsg-example-prod`
        resource_group `rg-example-eastus`
    OBJECT_END

    STATE st_nsg_active
        found boolean = true
        provisioning_state string = `Succeeded`
        security_rule_count int >= 1
    STATE_END

    CRI AND
        CTN az_nsg
            TEST all all AND
            STATE_REF st_nsg_active
            OBJECT_REF nsg_demo
        CTN_END
    CRI_END
DEF_END
